From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ole Algoritme <olealgoritme@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 19:49:26 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 13/13] nvk, nvk/rm: a compressible device-local memory type
 for images on GB20x

Patch 0011 (NVK_RM_COMPRESS_ALL) makes every plain device-local
allocation compressible, so vertex, index, indirect, uniform and storage
buffers end up in compressible memory as well.  NVIDIA's driver keeps
them apart with memory types instead, and so does this, behind
NVK_RM_COMPRESS_TYPE=1 (default off):

- A second DEVICE_LOCAL type on the VRAM heap, before the plain one so
  that allocators which take the first type an image allows (vkd3d-proton,
  DXVK) pick it for images.  Its memory is allocated
  NVKMD_MEM_COMPRESSIBLE (COMPR_ANY on RM).

- Images report it when they are optimal-tiling, not sparse, not
  protected, not host-transfer, not external and not video.  For a color
  format the spec does not let memoryTypeBits depend on the usage or the
  format, so sampled-only images may land there too; they are mapped with
  the compressible kind but not compressed, as with 0011.

- Buffers report it only when their usage is transfer-only and they are
  not sparse (the spec lets fewer usage bits allow more types).
  vkd3d-proton and DXVK put a transfer-only buffer over their image
  heaps and chunks to clear them; without it they would never use the
  type.  Vertex, index, indirect, uniform, storage and device-address
  buffers never do.  Generated-commands preprocess buffers, video
  session memory and imports do not either.

- A dedicated allocation of an image that is not compressed itself is
  never made compressible (nothing to gain; Helios may export it with the
  uncompressed layout); compressible images keep the dedicated path.
  Exported memory of the type is allocated uncompressed.

nvkmd_info gets the two policies, compress_all_mem (0011) and
has_compressed_mem_type, with has_compressible_mem meaning either.  The
counters of 0011 stay; a log line names the new type's index.
